  "answer_text": "A **Custodial IRA** (often referred to as an \"IRA for Kids\") is an individual retirement account opened and managed by an adult (the custodian) on behalf of a minor (the beneficiary) who has earned income. \n\nBecause minors cannot legally open investment accounts on their own, a custodial IRA allows parents, grandparents, or guardians to help children jumpstart their savings and leverage the power of decades of compound interest.\n\n---\n\n### How a Custodial IRA Works\n\n1. **Dual Roles (Custodian and Minor):** The adult opens the account and makes all the investment decisions. However, the minor is the actual owner of the assets.\n2. **The Earned Income Rule:** The child must have **earned income** to contribute. Earned income includes money from a W-2 job (like working at a grocery store or summer camp) or self-employment (like babysitting, mowing lawns, or tutoring). Money received from allowance, cash gifts, or investment returns does *not* qualify.\n3. **Age of Majority Transfer:** Once the child reaches the age of majority in their state (usually 18 or 21), the custody of the account is officially transferred to them. At that point, they gain full control over the funds.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Custodial IRAs\n\nLike standard individual retirement accounts, custodial IRAs come in two main varieties:\n\n* **Custodial Roth IRA (Most Common):** Contributions are made with after-tax money. Since minors generally earn very little and are in the lowest tax bracket, they gain almost no benefit from a tax deduction. Instead, a Roth IRA allows their investments to grow **100% tax-free**, and they can make tax-free withdrawals in retirement.\n* **Custodial Traditional IRA:** Contributions are made with pre-tax dollars, and taxes are paid when the money is withdrawn during retirement. This is rarely recommended for minors because their tax rate is already extremely low or zero.\n\n---\n\n### Contribution Limits\n\nThe amount you can contribute to a Custodial IRA is limited by two rules:\n1. **The child\u2019s earned income:** You cannot contribute more than the child actually earned that year. (For example, if a teenager earns $3,000 babysitting, the maximum contribution is $3,000). \n2. **The annual IRS limit:** Contributions cannot exceed the annual maximum set by the IRS:\n   * **2025 Limit:** $7,000\n   * **2026 Limit:** $7,500\n\n*Note: Parents or relatives can contribute to the child's IRA on their behalf (as a gift), as long as the total contributions do not exceed the child's total earned income for that year.*\n\n---\n\n### Key Benefits\n\n* **The Power of Time:** Because minors have 40 to 50 years before retirement, even small contributions made during their teenage years can grow exponentially. A few thousand dollars invested at age 15 can easily turn into hundreds of thousands by retirement.\n* **Flexibility of Roth Contributions:** With a Custodial Roth IRA, the *contributions* (the original money put in, not the earnings) can be withdrawn penalty-free and tax-free at any time. This can act as a backup fund for major life events, like a down payment on a first home or college expenses.\n* **Financial Literacy:** Opening a custodial IRA is an excellent way to teach children about the stock market, investing, budgeting, and compound interest from an early age.",
